      "text": "To determine the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\), we start by finding the prime factorization of \\(400\\).\n\nFirst, we note that:\n\\[ 400 = 20 \\times 20 = (4 \\times 5) \\times (4 \\times 5) = 2^4 \\times 5^2. \\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for the sum of the divisors of a number based on its prime factorization. If a number \\(n\\) has a prime factorization of \\(n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \\cdots p_k^{e_k}\\), then the sum of the divisors of \\(n\\) is given by:\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}). \\]\n\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2). \\]\n\nCalculating each term separately, we get:\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31, \\]\n\\[ 1 + 5 + 25 = 31. \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the prime factorization of \\(961\\). We check if \\(961\\) is a perfect square:\n\\[ 961 = 31^2. \\]\n\nThus, the prime factorization of \\(961\\) is \\(31^2\\). This means that the number \\(961\\) has only one distinct prime factor, which is \\(31\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1}. \\]",
