      "text": "To determine how many distinct prime factors the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) has, we first need to find the prime factorization of \\(400\\).\n\nStarting with \\(400\\):\n\\[\n400 = 4 \\times 100 = 2^2 \\times 100\n\\]\nNext, factorize \\(100\\):\n\\[\n100 = 10 \\times 10 = 10 \\times 10 = (2 \\times 5) \\times (2 \\times 5) = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\nThus, the prime factorization of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe formula for the sum of the positive divisors of a number \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2)\n\\]\nCalculate each sum separately:\n\\[\n1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 5 + 25 = 31\n\\]\n\nNow, multiply these results:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the prime factorization of \\(961\\):\n\\[\n961 = 31^2\n\\]\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(961\\) is \\(31^2\\), which shows that \\(961\\) has only two distinct prime factors: \\(31\\) and \\(31\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
