      "text": "First, we prime factorize $400$ into its prime factors:\n$400 = 2^4 \\cdot 5^2$.\n\nTo find the sum of the positive divisors of $400$, we use the formula:\n$\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\dots + p_1^{k_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\dots + p_2^{k_2}) \\dots (1 + p_n + p_n^2 + \\dots + p_n^{k_n})$,\nwhere $n = p_1^{k_1} p_2^{k_2} \\dots p_n^{k_n}$ is the prime factorization of $n$.\n\nFor $400 = 2^4 \\cdot 5^2$, we have:\n$\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) = (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) = 31 \\cdot 31 = 961$.\n\nNext, we factorize $961$ to find its prime factors:\n$961 = 31^2$.\n\nThe distinct prime factors of $961$ are $31$.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the positive divisors of $400$ has $\\boxed{1}$ distinct prime factor.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{1}$.",
